<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">verified_user</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Access Control</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            Access control works at three levels: the entity declares its role in authorization, a resource
            declares who may read or write its rows, and each property declares whether it is readable or
            writable, by which roles, scopes, and conditions.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Entity roles</h2>
    <p>
        Authorization starts with the entities. In an entity's <strong>Entity Identity</strong> section, the
        <strong>Type</strong> assigns its role in the authorization model:
    </p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Authorizable</strong> &mdash; the entity that authenticates: the account a request is made as.</li>
        <li><strong>Authorizable Role</strong> &mdash; the entity that carries roles, such as Moderator, Editor, or Viewer.</li>
        <li><strong>Authorization</strong> &mdash; the record that links an authorizable to its roles.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        These entity-level choices are what give the per-property roles and scopes below their meaning. Set
        them first &mdash; see <a class="help-link" href="#entities" data-help-anchor="entities">Working with Entities</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Resource controls</h2>
    <p>
        Select an entity to manage access controls on the resource as a whole. Add <strong>#Readable</strong>
        or <strong>#Writable</strong> entries from the entity's <strong>Access Control</strong> section. A
        resource-level control gates the whole row, not a single field:
    </p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>#Readable</strong> on an entity &mdash; rows the caller may not read are excluded from
            results at query time, so a listing omits them and a fetch by id returns nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>#Writable</strong> on an entity &mdash; a create, update, or delete of a row the caller
            may not write is rejected with <code>403 Forbidden</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Property controls</h2>
    <p>
        Select an attribute or relationship to manage its access controls. Add <strong>#Readable</strong>
        or <strong>#Writable</strong> entries from the access control toolbar, enable or disable them,
        and choose the scope. Property controls filter individual fields within a row the caller is already
        allowed to see &mdash; they apply after any resource control has admitted the row.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Roles and scopes</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Roles</strong> &mdash; add built-in roles such as Moderator, Editor, and Viewer, or create a custom role.</li>
        <li><strong>All</strong> &mdash; allows the role to access matching properties across all records.</li>
        <li><strong>Own</strong> &mdash; limits access to records owned by the current user or authorization context.</li>
        <li><strong>Owner relationships</strong> &mdash; mark the relationship that identifies ownership for generated access checks.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Conditions</h2>
    <p>
        Beyond roles and scope, a control can carry a <strong>condition</strong> &mdash; a predicate that
        must hold for access to be granted. Conditions are evaluated against the record, with these
        substitution variables available:
    </p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><code>$SUBJECT</code> &mdash; the authenticated account, e.g. <code>department == $SUBJECT.department</code>.</li>
        <li><code>$ENVIRONMENT</code> &mdash; the service environment.</li>
        <li><code>$REQUEST</code> &mdash; the incoming request: <code>$REQUEST.ip</code>, <code>$REQUEST.host</code>,
            and <code>$REQUEST.country</code> (an ISO country code, present only when the service has a
            geolocation resolver configured).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="help-subheading">When &mdash; time of access</h3>
    <p>
        Restrict access by time using predicate functions; no extra configuration is needed. For example,
        readable only during business hours:
    </p>
    <pre class="help-code"><code>hour(now()) &gt;= 9 AND hour(now()) &lt; 18</code></pre>
    <p>
        Other useful functions: <code>dayOfWeek(now())</code>, <code>date(now())</code>, and
        <code>dateSub(now(), 30, DAY)</code> for rolling windows.
    </p>

    <h3 class="help-subheading">Where &mdash; origin of access</h3>
    <p>
        Restrict access by where the request comes from. By network, matching a prefix of the client address:
    </p>
    <pre class="help-code"><code>$REQUEST.ip BEGINSWITH "10.0."</code></pre>
    <p>
        Or by country, when a geolocation resolver is configured &mdash; for example, readable only from
        Mexico during business hours:
    </p>
    <pre class="help-code"><code>$REQUEST.country == "MX" AND hour(now()) &gt;= 9 AND hour(now()) &lt; 18</code></pre>
    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">warning</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Origin is a signal, not a wall.</strong>
            Client addresses can be shared, proxied, or spoofed, and country lookups can be wrong. A
            country condition denies access whenever the country is unknown &mdash; it fails closed &mdash;
            but treat network and geo checks as one layer of defense, not the only one.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">lock</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Access control starts in the model.</strong>
            The generated service uses these declarations to decide which rows and fields can be read or
            written, by each role, scope, and condition.
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
